&= \frac{\sin 100^\circ + 4 \sin 100^\circ \cos 100^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ}.
\end{align*}
By double angle formula,
\[\frac{\sin 100^\circ + 4 \sin 100^\circ \cos 100^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} = \frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 200^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ}.\]

Then by sum-to-product,
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 200^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} &= \frac{\sin 100^\circ + \sin 200^\circ + \sin 200^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} \\
&= \frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 200^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} = \frac{2 \sin 150^\circ \cos 50^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} \\
&= \frac{\cos 10^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} = \boxed{1}.
\end{align*}

We start by expressing the given expression in terms of sine and cosine:
\begin{align*}
\tan 100^\circ + 4 \sin 100^\circ &= \frac{\sin 100^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} + 4 \sin 100^\circ \\
&= \frac{\sin 100^\circ + 4 \sin 100^\circ \cos 100^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ}.
\end{align*}

Using the double angle formula, we know that $\sin 2\theta = 2 \sin \theta \cos \theta$. Therefore, $4 \sin 100^\circ \cos 100^\circ = 2 \sin 200^\circ$.

Thus,
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin 100^\circ + 4 \sin 100^\circ \cos 100^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} &= \frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 200^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ}.
\end{align*}

Using the angle addition formula, $\sin(180^\circ + \theta) = -\sin \theta$, we get:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 200^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} &= \frac{\sin 100^\circ - 2 \sin (-20^\circ)}{\cos 100^\circ} \\
&= \frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 20^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ}.
\end{align*}

Using the sum-to-product identities, $\sin A + \sin B = 2 \sin \left(\frac{A+B}{2}\right) \cos \left(\frac{A-B}{2}\right)$, we get:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin 100^\circ + 2 \sin 20^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} &= \frac{2 \sin 60^\circ \cos 40^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ}.
\end{align*}

Since $\cos 100^\circ = -\cos 80^\circ$ and $\cos 80^\circ = \cos (90^\circ - 10^\circ) = \sin 10^\circ$, we have:
\begin{align*}
\frac{2 \sin 60^\circ \cos 40^\circ}{\cos 100^\circ} &= \frac{2 \sin 60^\circ \cos 40^\circ}{-\sin 10^\circ}.
\end{align*}

Using the identity $\sin 60^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we get:
\begin{align*}
\frac{2 \sin 60^\circ \cos 40^\circ}{-\sin 10^\circ} &= \frac{\sqrt{3} \cos 40^\circ}{-\sin 10^\circ}.
\end{align*}

Thus, the simplified expression is:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{\frac{\sqrt{3} \cos 40^\circ}{-\sin 10^\circ}}.
\end{align*}

